I'll implement the robot naming functionality based on the requirements. Let me 
analyze what needs to be done:



1. Generate random names in format: 2 uppercase letters + 3 digits (e.g., 
"RX837")

2. Ensure unique names across all robots

3. Provide functionality to reset a robot to factory settings (wipe name)

4. Only use standard libraries



Here's my implementation:



robot_name.h

```diff
@@ -1,8 +1,23 @@
 #if !defined(ROBOT_NAME_H)
 #define ROBOT_NAME_H
 
+#include <string>
+#include <unordered_set>
+
 namespace robot_name {
+
+class Robot {
+public:
+    Robot();
+    std::string get_name();
+    void reset();
+
+private:
+    std::string name;
+    static std::unordered_set<std::string> used_names;
+    static void generate_name();
+};
 
 }  // namespace robot_name
 
-#endif // ROBOT_NAME_H+#endif // ROBOT_NAME_H
```



robot_name.cpp

```diff
@@ -1,5 +1,48 @@
 #include "robot_name.h"
+#include <random>
+#include <ctime>
 
 namespace robot_name {
 
+std::unordered_set<std::string> Robot::used_names;
+
+Robot::Robot() {
+    generate_name();
+}
+
+std::string Robot::get_name() {
+    return name;
+}
+
+void Robot::reset() {
+    used_names.erase(name);
+    name.clear();
+    generate_name();
+}
+
+void Robot::generate_name() {
+    const std::string letters = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";
+    const std::string digits = "0123456789";
+
+    std::mt19937 rng(static_cast<unsigned int>(std::time(nullptr)));
+
+    std::string new_name;
+    do {
+        // Generate 2 random letters
+        std::uniform_int_distribution<int> letter_dist(0, letters.size() - 1);
+        new_name += letters[letter_dist(rng)];
+        new_name += letters[letter_dist(rng)];
+
+        // Generate 3 random digits
+        std::uniform_int_distribution<int> digit_dist(0, digits.size() - 1);
+        new_name += digits[digit_dist(rng)];
+        new_name += digits[digit_dist(rng)];
+        new_name += digits[digit_dist(rng)];
+
+    } while (used_names.find(new_name) != used_names.end());
+
+    name = new_name;
+    used_names.insert(new_name);
+}
+
 }  // namespace robot_name
```



This implementation:



1. Creates a `Robot` class that manages its own name

2. Uses a static `unordered_set` to track all used names and ensure uniqueness

3. Generates random names in the required format (2 letters + 3 digits)

4. Provides `get_name()` to retrieve the current name

5. Provides `reset()` to wipe the name and generate a new unique one

6. Uses standard C++ libraries only (random, ctime, string, unordered_set)

7. The random number generator is seeded with current time to ensure randomness

8. The name generation loop continues until a unique name is found



The solution handles all the requirements:

- First time a robot is created, it gets a random name

- Names follow the specified format

- Names are unique across all robots

- Reset functionality wipes the name and generates a new unique one

- No external dependencies beyond standard library

